---
// Hand-authored inline SVG: five federal identity mechanisms used across the
// US immigration system, compared with what exists for a US citizen. Inline
// for the same reasons as ThreeQuestions.astro — see that file's header.
//
// THE RIGHT-HAND COLUMN IS A COMPOSITE, NOT ONE PERSON'S PATH. A visitor, a
// student and a permanent resident do not encounter the same systems. The
// diagram asks what the federal immigration system has built across those
// statuses; its caption and labels must never imply that every non-citizen gets
// an A-number, uses SEVIS and files I-407. That distinction is now stated in
// the prose immediately before and after the drawing.
//
// Row -> supporting sentence in the post:
//   identifier    "Many people who pass through USCIS also receive a
//                  purpose-built identifier: the A-number… It is not universal."
//   registration  "Many visitors and students satisfy the registration
//                  requirement through a visa or Form I-94 without receiving
//                  one. In April 2025, DHS created Form G-325R so people missed
//                  by the existing forms finally had a way to comply…"
//   address       "Form AR-11 requires a change of address within 10 days of
//                  moving (8 U.S.C. § 1305), and failing to file is a criminal
//                  offense"
//                 Both cells in this row were reworded after a galley note
//                 (hw, Sept 2026). The duty cell read "8 U.S.C. § 1305, a
//                 criminal offense", which left the offence attached to
//                 Form AR-11 or to moving rather than to NOT filing — the
//                 drawing was less precise than the sentence it transcribes.
//                 Name the omission, not the form. The citizen cell read
//                 "none — and no register to write it to", database voice in
//                 the row a lay reader meets first. Keep both halves: no duty,
//                 and nowhere it could be recorded.
//   status        "for students and exchange visitors, the Student and Exchange
//                  Visitor Information System (SEVIS) does the same job, with
//                  the school reporting on their behalf"
//   exit          "A permanent resident who gives up that status… can file Form
//                  I-407, and USCIS reports the departure to the IRS."
//   carry note    8 U.S.C. § 1304(e), which closes "What proves it": a
//                 registered non-citizen 18 or older must carry the document;
//                 no US citizen has a comparable duty.
//
// The citizen column is dashed and empty in four rows on purpose. Filling it
// with near-equivalents (passport, voter roll, DMV record) would turn the
// proxies the post criticises into a coherent structure it says does not exist.
// The SSN cell stays solid because the number exists, though it was not designed
// as a general identity credential.
//
// Styles live in global.css (.post-diagram, .dg-*) per the one-stylesheet rule.
// aria IDs are prefixed dg-asym-* — four diagrams share this page.
---

<figure class="post-figure post-diagram">
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 700 462"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby="dg-asym-title dg-asym-desc"
    class="dg"
  >
    <title id="dg-asym-title">
      Five identity mechanisms across the US immigration system, compared with a US citizen
    </title>
    <desc id="dg-asym-desc">
      Five rows — stable identifier, registration on arrival, change of address,
      status reporting, exit record — across two columns, US citizen and the US
      immigration system. The immigration column is explicitly a composite
      across different statuses, not one non-citizen's path: A-numbers are not
      universal, registration can happen through a visa, I-94 or G-325R, SEVIS
      covers students and exchange visitors, and I-407 records a permanent
      resident giving up that status. The citizen column holds only the Social
      Security number; the other four cells are drawn dashed and empty because
      there is no connected citizen equivalent. A note adds that a registered
      non-citizen eighteen or older must carry evidence of registration, while
      no citizen has a comparable duty.
    </desc>

    {/* ---- column headers ---- */}
    <text x="334" y="24" class="dg-call">US citizen</text>
    <text x="577" y="24" class="dg-call">US immigration</text>

    <path class="dg-edge" d="M6 34 H 694" />

    {/* ---- row labels ---- */}
    <text x="6" y="82" class="dg-call dg-call--start">stable identifier</text>
    <text x="6" y="154" class="dg-call dg-call--start">registration on arrival</text>
    <text x="6" y="226" class="dg-call dg-call--start">change of address</text>
    <text x="6" y="298" class="dg-call dg-call--start">status reporting</text>
    <text x="6" y="370" class="dg-call dg-call--start">exit record</text>

    {/* ---- row 1: the one cell the citizen column actually has ---- */}
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="220" y="46" width="228" height="64" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="334" y="70" class="dg-label">SSN</text>
      <text x="334" y="90" class="dg-sub">“NOT FOR IDENTIFICATION”,</text>
      <text x="334" y="104" class="dg-sub">printed on the card 1946–1972</text>
    </g>
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="460" y="46" width="234" height="64" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="577" y="70" class="dg-label">A-number</text>
      <text x="577" y="90" class="dg-sub">assigned in many USCIS cases;</text>
      <text x="577" y="104" class="dg-sub">not universal</text>
    </g>

    {/* ---- row 2: registration on arrival ---- */}
    <g class="dg-group dg-group--out">
      <rect x="220" y="118" width="228" height="64" class="dg-node dg-node--out" />
      <text x="334" y="156" class="dg-note">none</text>
    </g>
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="460" y="118" width="234" height="64" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="577" y="142" class="dg-label">visa · I-94 · G-325R</text>
      <text x="577" y="162" class="dg-sub">different routes feed the</text>
      <text x="577" y="176" class="dg-sub">federal registration system</text>
    </g>

    {/* ---- row 3: change of address ---- */}
    <g class="dg-group dg-group--out">
      <rect x="220" y="190" width="228" height="64" class="dg-node dg-node--out" />
      <text x="334" y="222" class="dg-note">none — and nowhere</text>
      <text x="334" y="238" class="dg-note">to register it</text>
    </g>
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="460" y="190" width="234" height="64" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="577" y="214" class="dg-label">Form AR-11</text>
      <text x="577" y="234" class="dg-sub">within 10 days of moving;</text>
      <text x="577" y="248" class="dg-sub">not filing is a crime — 8 U.S.C. § 1305</text>
    </g>

    {/* ---- row 4: status reporting ---- */}
    <g class="dg-group dg-group--out">
      <rect x="220" y="262" width="228" height="64" class="dg-node dg-node--out" />
      <text x="334" y="300" class="dg-note">none</text>
    </g>
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="460" y="262" width="234" height="64" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="577" y="286" class="dg-label">SEVIS</text>
      <text x="577" y="306" class="dg-sub">students and exchange visitors;</text>
      <text x="577" y="320" class="dg-sub">reported by the school</text>
    </g>

    {/* ---- row 5: exit record ---- */}
    <g class="dg-group dg-group--out">
      <rect x="220" y="334" width="228" height="64" class="dg-node dg-node--out" />
      <text x="334" y="366" class="dg-note">none — the IRS finds out,</text>
      <text x="334" y="382" class="dg-note">and tells no one else</text>
    </g>
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="460" y="334" width="234" height="64" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="577" y="358" class="dg-label">Form I-407</text>
      <text x="577" y="378" class="dg-sub">permanent residents giving up</text>
      <text x="577" y="392" class="dg-sub">status; USCIS tells the IRS</text>
    </g>

    <text x="6" y="428" class="dg-note dg-note--start">
      And 8 U.S.C. § 1304(e): a registered non-citizen 18 or older must carry the document at all times.
    </text>
    <text x="6" y="444" class="dg-note dg-note--start">
      No US citizen has any comparable duty.
    </text>
  </svg>

  <figcaption>
    The right-hand column gathers mechanisms used across different immigration
    statuses; no single person encounters every row. Taken together, they show
    connections the federal government built for immigration but not for its
    citizens. The dashed cells are empty on purpose: there is no connected
    citizen equivalent to put in them.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
